Fix the detached-entity path of onFlushDirty(): keep the temporary session open while the
previous state is compared, and handle a detached entity with no stored row.

openmrs-module-auditlog (master ba96ba5, and the 1.1 release) handles an update to a
DETACHED entity by loading its previous state in a temporary session. It closed that
session as soon as the state was read, and only then compared each previous value with
the current one. A previous value that is a lazy association is a proxy bound to the
closed session; OpenMRS's BaseOpenmrsObject.equals() initialises it, and Hibernate
throws LazyInitializationException. That exception escapes onFlushDirty() and fails the
caller's own save.

Seen on a LiberiaEMR facility stack on core 2.8.8: Initializer, which saves detached
metadata, failed to load a program workflow ("could not initialize proxy
[org.openmrs.Concept#4017] - no Session") and an appointment service type ("...
[org.openmrs.module.appointments.model.Speciality#1] - no Session"). Neither fails
without the module.

The same path also assumed a stored row always exists. Making an existing Person a
Patient saves a detached Patient whose id has no patient row yet: the temporary session's
get() returns null and getEntityPersister(null, null) throws NullPointerException, which
also fails the caller's save. Seen on the same stack: creating a patient over REST for an
existing person, and Initializer loading an appointment service type.

The session now closes in a finally after the comparison and serialization, and a missing
row means no previous state, so every current value is recorded as the change. The same
holds when the person is still in the second-level cache: get() then throws
WrongClassException instead of returning null, and that is also read as no previous state.
This diff is the one proposed upstream (branch detached-previous-state), main code only.

Applied by distribution/backend/Dockerfile (stage 2b). Tracked in
packages/modify-pr/.patches/0003-openmrs-module-auditlog-detached-previous-state.md.

